RESEARCH ONLY — no code changed. This is a scoping/planning write-up; the actual
feature is deferred until you've reviewed it and answered the open questions at
the end.

What this is
------------
"Pre-render the whole article" = run the existing normalize-then-synthesize
pipeline for every paragraph, but instead of playing each WAV as it finishes,
keep going and produce one compact audio file the user can keep offline and
play later without re-rendering. It also resolves ticket 02 for any article
that has been pre-rendered: paragraph-boundary synthesis happens up front in
the background instead of live during playback.

Where it plugs in
-----------------
Today SpeechController renders one paragraph to WAV with
TextToSpeech.synthesizeToFile, plays it with MediaPlayer, and (since ticket 02)
pre-renders the next paragraph while the current one plays. The reading screen's
left margin + scroll depend on knowing which paragraph is audible, so any
pre-render scheme must keep a per-paragraph -> time-offset mapping.

Format: probably AAC-in-M4A, no new dependency
---------------------------------------------
- Android's platform codecs do NOT include an MPEG-1 Layer III (*MP3*) encoder.
  MediaCodec can decode MP3 but cannot write it, so a universal ".mp3" only works
  if we ship an encoder — a dependency we'd rather avoid.
- The platform DOES ship an AAC-LC encoder (the standard "OMX.google.aac.encoder")
  plus MediaMuxer, which muxes AAC into an .m4a (MPEG-4) container. AAC-in-M4A is
  playable on essentially every Android device, needs zero extra libraries, and
  is a reasonable size/quality trade-off. This should be the default.
- Distant option: Opus (better size/quality), but the platform Opus encoder isn't
  guaranteed on every device and MediaMuxer has no Ogg muxer, so it would pull in
  a library. Park this unless file size becomes a problem.
- WAV can stay as the intermediate: TextToSpeech has no portable way to synthesize
  straight to compressed audio, so the likely pipeline is synthesizeToFile -> WAV
  per paragraph, then decode WAV -> encode AAC -> mux, appending each paragraph's
  start time (ms) to a cue list.

Keeping the paragraph highlight in sync
---------------------------------------
Two viable shapes:
  A) ONE file + a sidecar cue table (paragraph index -> start ms). Requires the
     encoder/muxer timestamps to line up with a monotonic clock and the player to
     report position reliably (MediaPlayer/ExoPlayer both do). This is the nicer
     user experience (one artifact to keep/download).
  B) Per-paragraph AAC files in a folder, indexed by a small JSON manifest. Sync
     is trivial (one file = one paragraph) and it reuses the current playback,
     but there's no single "downloadable" file and cross-article seek is coarser.

(A) is where adopting ExoPlayer (media3) pays off (see below); (B) is less code
now.

Is this the moment to adopt ExoPlayer?
-------------------------------------
Ticket 09 already flagged ExoPlayer as a desirable, more podcast-like engine.
Pre-render strengthens the case: ExoPlayer gives precise seeking (so cue offsets
work), background playback, and MediaSession/notification integration, and would
let us drop the per-paragraph MediaPlayer dance for pre-rendered files. It is a
real dependency, though. Two-step option: ship (B) on the current MediaPlayer
first, then move to ExoPlayer when we want single-file (A).

Where the button lives
----------------------
Your instinct (the bookmarks screen) is right: a pre-rendered article is basically
"a page saved for offline listening", which is adjacent to a bookmark. Suggested
shape: on the Bookmarks screen add an action per entry ("pre-render / make
available offline") that starts a foreground service with a progress bar, because
rendering a long article can take minutes on a slow engine. Store the files in
app-private storage so no storage permission is needed; delete them on the
existing "remove bookmark" flow.

